7. LIKE operator

It is a type of special operator which is used for pattern matching.

--> always pattern matching must be written using wild card operators

--> Always pattern must written within single quotes.

syntax:
Column_Name LIKE 'pattern_to_match'

--> following are the wild card operator used for writing the pattern.

%(percentile)

(underscore)

%(percentile)

It is a symbol which accepts the any number of character

---> It accepts any character

--->It accepts even no character

_(Underscore):

It is accept any character

It accepts only one character at a time.

---waqtd names of an employees who are having 2 times character 'R' in there name

select ename
from emp
where ename like '%R%R%';

---waqtd names of an employees who are having consecutive 'T' in there name.

select ename
from emp
where ename like '%TT%';

---waqtd name, and hiredate of an employees who are hired in the year 87

select ename, hiredate
from emp
where hiredate like '%87';

SQL> ---waqtd name job and hiredate of an employees who are hired of the date 21 without bothering about the month and the year

SQL> select ename, job, hiredate
from emp
where hiredate like '21%';

8.NOT LIKE operator:

It is a type of special operator similar to LIKE operator but instead of select the pattern it used to reject the pattern.

syntax:
Column_Name NOT LIKE 'pattern_to_reject';

---waqtd name and job of an employees except those employees whose name starts with 'A'letter

select ename,job
from emp
where ename not like 'A%';

---waqtd name of an employees except those employees who are having character 'T' in there name

select ename
from emp
where ename not like '%T%';
